WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
Welcome to Grade 7 English, Unit 9:
The Digital World and Future Innovation.

2
00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:10.920
In this unit you will explore
how digital technology, robotics,

3
00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:16.840
and innovative farming are reshaping daily
life across East Africa and the wider world.

4
00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:20.720
You will read about young people
who use smartphones, coding skills,

5
00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:24.120
and creative thinking to solve
real problems in their communities.

6
00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:27.760
Through reading, writing, speaking, and grammar activities,

7
00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:33.420
you will sharpen your English skills while thinking
critically about the role of technology in our lives.

8
00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:39.600
In this short lecture, preview the key
ideas and prepare for the independent lesson.

9
00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:41.600
By the end of this unit,

10
00:00:42.020 --> 00:00:48.980
you will be able to read and understand a passage about
digital innovation and discuss its ideas confidently in English.

11
00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:57.220
You will also use reported speech to relay what someone
has said: “She said that the app had just been updated.”

12
00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:05.580
You will also form conditional sentences with if and
unless: “If we use technology wisely, it will help us.”

13
00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:08.520
Keep these goals in mind as you work.

14
00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:16.200
Your vocabulary work begins with these words: significance,
specialisation, stability, ellipsis, substitution,

15
00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:17.180
algorithm.

16
00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:23.240
Listen to each pronunciation in the
vocabulary lab, study its meaning in context,

17
00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:26.980
and use the word accurately in
your own discussion and writing.

18
00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:34.100
The reading sequence includes Smart Farms and Digital
Dreams and A Radio Interview: The School Coding Club.

19
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:35.740
Read actively.

20
00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:40.920
Identify each text's central ideas,
notice viewpoint and reliability,

21
00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:44.780
and support every inference with
relevant evidence from the text.

22
00:01:45.540 --> 00:01:50.300
The first language focus is Present
Perfect with Just, and Reported Speech.

23
00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:55.180
The present perfect with just shows
that an action happened only moments ago,

24
00:01:55.720 --> 00:02:00.280
while reported speech lets us relay what someone
said by shifting the tense back one step —

25
00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:05.760
two patterns the digital world uses constantly
for fresh updates and second-hand news.

26
00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:12.000
Notice the structure in the model examples, then apply
it deliberately in connected sentences and paragraphs.

27
00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:17.320
In speaking, you will complete
Speaking 1: Vocabulary Expert Interview.

28
00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:22.880
Organise your ideas, use evidence
and respond clearly to questions.

29
00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:26.660
In writing, you will plan, draft, check and revise.

30
00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:30.440
Use the success criteria before submitting your work.

31
00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:32.160
You are ready to begin.

32
00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:39.360
Start with the vocabulary lab, continue through
reading and comprehension, apply the grammar focus,

33
00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:42.080
and complete the speaking and writing practices.

34
00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:49.820
Use the AI tutor for hints and feedback any time, and
bring your questions to a live session too if you join one.
